         { "a": "NewZealand", "b": "USSR", "value": 15.0 },
         { "a": "NewZealand", "b": "France", "value": -30.0 }
       ]
+    },
+    {
+      "note": [
+        "The Horn and the Great Lakes, block 1 of 4: Somalia. The line that decides this block is Ethiopia, and it is entered at only -15 rather than at the deep negative the Ogaden war would suggest, because January 1990 catches this dyad at the warmest point it reached between 1960 and 1988. Mogadishu invaded the Ogaden in July 1977 and was thrown out in March 1978 by an Ethiopian army with 15,000 Cuban troops and a Soviet airlift behind it, and each government then spent a decade hosting and arming the other's insurgents - Addis Ababa the Somali National Movement and the Somali Salvation Democratic Front, Mogadishu the Western Somali Liberation Front. Then, on 3 April 1988, Siad Barre and Mengistu signed the accord that restored diplomatic relations, exchanged prisoners of war held since 1977, withdrew both armies from the frontier and ended the sponsorship on both sides. Neither of them did it out of goodwill; each did it because he could no longer fight his own insurgency and the other man's at once, and within seven weeks the SNM, evicted from its Ethiopian sanctuaries, had gone into Somalia and taken Hargeisa and Burao. That is a real detente between two governments that had stopped being able to afford each other, and -15 is what a border with an unsettled claim across it and no shooting on it looks like. It is entered negative rather than neutral because the Ogaden claim in nations.rs is still constitutionally live.",
+        "The United States at 20 is an alliance in the last weeks of its life. Somalia gave the Americans access to Berbera - the port and airstrip the Soviet navy had built - under the agreement of 22 August 1980, and took something over $800m of economic and military assistance across the decade in exchange. Then came Hargeisa: the aid was suspended in 1988 and cut off by Congress in 1989 over the bombing of Somalia's own cities. 20 is what is left of a client relationship that Washington has already stopped paying for and has not yet renounced.",
+        "The Soviet Union at -25 is the mirror image. Moscow armed Somalia from 1963, built Berbera, and then switched sides in the middle of the Ogaden war; Barre abrogated the friendship treaty and expelled every Soviet adviser on 13 November 1977. Italy at 50 is the highest line Somalia has and it is colonial in origin and fiscal in practice - the trusteeship power in the south until 1960, the largest bilateral donor by a wide margin through the eighties, and the government in Rome that kept underwriting Barre after Washington stopped.",
+        "Egypt and Saudi Arabia in the forties are the Arab League, which Somalia joined on 14 February 1974 and which is most of why a Horn state's diplomacy ran north rather than south. Riyadh mattered concretely: Saudi Arabia bought the live sheep and goats that were Somalia's largest export, and its periodic import bans over rinderpest were economic events in Somalia. Kenya at 15 is deliberately positive - the shifta war ended in 1967, Barre gave up the claim on Kenyan territory in 1981, and the two governments got on rather better than either got on with Addis Ababa."
+      ],
+      "pairs": [
+        { "a": "Somalia", "b": "Ethiopia", "value": -15.0 },
+        { "a": "Somalia", "b": "Italy", "value": 50.0 },
+        { "a": "Somalia", "b": "Egypt", "value": 45.0 },
+        { "a": "Somalia", "b": "SaudiArabia", "value": 40.0 },
+        { "a": "Somalia", "b": "Djibouti", "value": 30.0 },
+        { "a": "Somalia", "b": "China", "value": 30.0 },
+        { "a": "Somalia", "b": "USA", "value": 20.0 },
+        { "a": "Somalia", "b": "Kenya", "value": 15.0 },
+        { "a": "Somalia", "b": "Libya", "value": -15.0 },
+        { "a": "Somalia", "b": "USSR", "value": -25.0 }
+      ]
+    },
+    {
+      "note": [
+        "Block 2 of 4: Djibouti, whose foreign policy is one number and the rest is commentary. France at 75 is the highest line either of the two Horn states in this batch carries and it is not sentiment: the defence and cooperation accords signed on 27 June 1977, the day of independence, kept roughly 3,800 French troops in the country - the 13e Demi-brigade de Legion Etrangere, a marine regiment and a fighter squadron - and made Djibouti the largest French garrison outside France. That garrison was Djibouti's army, its border guarantee against two neighbours who had each claimed it, and a substantial share of its GDP in rent and local spending.",
+        "Ethiopia at 35 is the railway. The Chemin de Fer Djibouto-Ethiopien is the reason the colony existed and the reason the country is solvent: it was Ethiopia's outlet to the sea, and it stayed so through everything until 1998, when the Eritrean war made Djibouti Ethiopia's ONLY outlet. Hassan Gouled Aptidon hosted and brokered the Ethiopia-Somalia normalisation talks of April 1988 in his own capital, which is the small-state diplomacy this number is paying for.",
+        "The Arab League lines - Saudi Arabia 40, Egypt 30 - follow Djibouti's accession in September 1977 and the Gulf money that came with it. The Soviet Union at 5 is not a relationship at all: a state with a French air wing on its soil was never going to have one, and it is entered barely positive rather than negative because Djibouti had no quarrel with Moscow either."
+      ],
+      "pairs": [
+        { "a": "Djibouti", "b": "France", "value": 75.0 },
+        { "a": "Djibouti", "b": "SaudiArabia", "value": 40.0 },
+        { "a": "Djibouti", "b": "Ethiopia", "value": 35.0 },
+        { "a": "Djibouti", "b": "Egypt", "value": 30.0 },
+        { "a": "Djibouti", "b": "USA", "value": 25.0 },
+        { "a": "Djibouti", "b": "Italy", "value": 20.0 },
+        { "a": "Djibouti", "b": "Kenya", "value": 20.0 },
+        { "a": "Djibouti", "b": "USSR", "value": 5.0 }
+      ]
+    },
+    {
+      "note": [
+        "Block 3 of 4: Rwanda, and the number to look at is Uganda at 10. Every other line here is warm, and the cold one is with the neighbour Rwanda's government was on the best formal terms with. Habyarimana and Museveni were allies in public; the substance underneath was that roughly half a million Rwandan Tutsi refugees and their children had been in Uganda since 1959-64, that Kampala's parliament had moved in 1990 to strip them of the land rights they had acquired, that Rwanda refused to take them back on the stated ground that the country was full - and that several thousand of them were serving officers and men of Museveni's National Resistance Army, Fred Rwigyema having been its deputy commander and Paul Kagame its head of military intelligence. 10 is two governments being correct with each other over a fault line, and the sim should be able to reach 1 October 1990 from it without being told to.",
+        "France at 55 and Belgium at 55 are the two European patrons and they are not the same relationship. Belgium is the former trustee, the largest aid donor, the training mission and the university; France is the military cooperation agreement of 18 July 1975 and a personal channel from Kigali into the Elysee, which is what produced Operation Noroit within seventy-two hours of the RPF crossing the border. Zaire at 50 is the Communaute Economique des Pays des Grands Lacs, founded at Gisenyi on 20 September 1976, and Mobutu's standing offer of the Division Speciale Presidentielle to any Great Lakes government that needed it - an offer taken up in October 1990.",
+        "Burundi at 15 is low for two countries with the same language, the same size and a shared border, and that is the point: each was the other's demonstration of what happens when the other community holds the state, and each hosted the other's refugees. Tanzania at 30 and Kenya at 25 are the ports - a landlocked country's fuel and coffee move through Dar es Salaam and Mombasa, and that is an economic dependency before it is a friendship."
+      ],
+      "pairs": [
+        { "a": "Rwanda", "b": "Belgium", "value": 55.0 },
+        { "a": "Rwanda", "b": "France", "value": 55.0 },
+        { "a": "Rwanda", "b": "Zaire", "value": 50.0 },
+        { "a": "Rwanda", "b": "Germany", "value": 30.0 },
+        { "a": "Rwanda", "b": "Tanzania", "value": 30.0 },
+        { "a": "Rwanda", "b": "Kenya", "value": 25.0 },
+        { "a": "Rwanda", "b": "USA", "value": 25.0 },
+        { "a": "Rwanda", "b": "China", "value": 25.0 },
+        { "a": "Rwanda", "b": "Burundi", "value": 15.0 },
+        { "a": "Rwanda", "b": "Uganda", "value": 10.0 }
+      ]
+    },
+    {
+      "note": [
+        "Block 4 of 4: Burundi, which is Rwanda's diplomacy with the weights moved. Belgium at 45 and France at 40 sit below Kigali's 55s because Buyoya's Burundi kept a deliberate distance from both: Bujumbura had broken with Brussels for a period in the seventies, and the Franco-African summit circuit was Habyarimana's world rather than his.",
+        "China at 35 is the highest non-European line in this block and it is concrete. Burundi recognised the People's Republic early, the relationship survived a rupture in 1965 and was restored in 1971, and Chinese construction teams built the trunk roads out of Bujumbura, the presidential palace and the Gitega works - the standard Chinese package of the period, in a country too small for anyone else to bother with. Zaire at 45 is the CEPGL again, from the other member.",
+        "Tanzania at 10 is the coldest line either Great Lakes state carries and it is not about trade. Something over 150,000 Burundian Hutu refugees from the killings of 1972 were still in camps in Kigoma and Rukwa in 1990; Palipehutu had been founded in those camps in 1980 and organised in them for a decade; and Dar es Salaam declined to police them. Burundi's government regarded Tanzania as a sanctuary for an armed opposition, which is exactly what Ethiopia and Somalia thought of each other in block 1 - the same object, twenty points warmer because nobody had invaded anybody."
+      ],
+      "pairs": [
+        { "a": "Burundi", "b": "Zaire", "value": 45.0 },
+        { "a": "Burundi", "b": "Belgium", "value": 45.0 },
+        { "a": "Burundi", "b": "France", "value": 40.0 },
+        { "a": "Burundi", "b": "China", "value": 35.0 },
+        { "a": "Burundi", "b": "USA", "value": 20.0 },
+        { "a": "Burundi", "b": "Kenya", "value": 20.0 },
+        { "a": "Burundi", "b": "Uganda", "value": 15.0 },
+        { "a": "Burundi", "b": "Tanzania", "value": 10.0 }
+      ]
     }
   ]
 }
